{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And the man came into the house</b> — Eliezer is the man who came. \"And he unloaded the camels\" turns back to refer to Laban, for he conducted himself with his guests according to proper manners and unloaded their camels, and gave them straw and provender, and gave water to wash the feet of Eliezer and the feet of the men who were with him. For it is far-fetched that Eliezer himself would be the one giving water to wash his own feet and the feet of his men. And similarly, \"And there passed by men, Midianite merchants, and they drew and lifted up Joseph from the pit\" (Genesis 37:28) — for \"and they drew\" turns back to his brothers who are mentioned in the first verse. And similarly, \"And Ziba said to the king, According to all that my master the king commands his servant, so shall your servant do; and Mephibosheth eats at my table as one of the king's sons\" (II Samuel 9:11) — and these are the words of David. And many similar cases. And the meaning of \"וַיְפַתַּח הַגְּמַלִּים\" is that he loosened the halters of their necks, for the custom is to lead them tied, or they went girded to the saddle of the riding seat upon them, in the language of \"Let not him who girds on his armor boast as he who takes it off\" (I Kings 20:11), \"Loose yourself from the bonds of your neck\" (Isaiah 52:2). And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 24:32) that he untied their muzzle, which was sealing their mouths so that they would not graze in others' fields. And the language of Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 60:8): \"He untied their muzzles\" — Rabbi Huna and Rabbi Jeremiah asked Rabbi Hiyya bar Abba: Were not the camels of Abraham our father comparable to the donkey of Rabbi Pinchas ben Yair, etc. — and this question is intended to refute the untying of the muzzle, for it is impossible that the piety in the house of Rabbi Pinchas ben Yair could be greater than in the house of Abraham our father. And since the donkey of Rabbi Pinchas ben Yair did not need to be guarded from forbidden things — that its owners might feed it — how much more so the camels of Abraham our father, and they have no need for a muzzle, for no iniquity will befall the righteous."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "Ramban cites the well-known midrashic episode (Hullin 7b; Genesis Rabbah 60:8) regarding Rabbi Pinchas ben Yair's donkey, which would not eat untithed produce."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:34:29.471869893Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}